Dataset and Dataloader are core abstractions in PyTorch that decouple how you define your data from how you efficiently iterate over it in training loops.

Dataset Class
The Dataset class is essentially a blueprint. When you create a custom Dataset, you decide how data is loaded and returned.
It defines:
__init__() - which tells how data should be loaded.
__len__() - which returns the total number of samples.
__getitem__(index) - which returns the data (and lable) at the given index.


DataLoader Class
The DataLoader wraps a Dataset and handles batching, shuffling, and parallel loading for you.

DataLoader Control Flow:
At the start of each epoch, the DataLoader(if shuffle=True) shuffles indices(using a sampler).
It divides the indices into chunks of batch_size.
For each index in the chunk, data samples are fetched from the Dataset object.
The samples are then collected and combined into a batch(using a collate_fn).
The batch is returned to the main training loop(pipeline).



Sampler
In PyTorch, the sampler in the DataLoader determines the strategy for selecting samples from the dataset during data loading. It controls how indices of the dataset are drawn for each batch.


Types of Sampler
PyTorch provides several predefined samples, and you can create custom ones:
SequentialSampler- Samples elements sequentially, in the order they appear in the dataset. Default when shuffle=False.
RandomSampler- Samples elements randomly without replacement. Default when shuffle=True



collate_fn()
The collate_fn in PyTorch's DataLoader is a function that specifies how to combine a list of samples form a dataset into a single batch. By default, the DataLoader uses a simple batch collation mechanism, but collate_fn allows you to customize how the data should be processed and batched.



DataLoader Important Parameters
 *`dataset` → dataset object to load data from
 *`batch\\\\\\\_size` → number of samples per batch
 *`shuffle` → randomly shuffle data every epoch
 *`num\\\\\\\_workers` → number of subprocesses used for loading data
 *`drop\\\\\\\_last` → drops the last incomplete batch
 *`pin\\\\\\\_memory` → speeds up CPU-to-GPU data transfer
 *`sampler` → custom strategy for selecting samples
 *`collate\\\\\\\_fn` → custom function for creating batches
 `persistent\\\\\\\_workers` → keeps worker processes alive between epochs
 `timeout` → maximum wait time for worker processes
 `worker\\\\\\\_init\\\\\\\_fn` → function to initialize each worker process
 `generator` → controls randomness for reproducibility
 `prefetch\\\\\\\_factor` → number of batches loaded in advance by each worker
 `batch\\\\\\\_sampler` → returns batches of indices directly instead of single indices
